      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During Prophase, the chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes, a crucial step to learn about genetic organization. Next, in Metaphase, these chromosomes line up neatly along the cell's equator, helping students understand proper alignment for learning. Anaphase involves the sister chromatids separating and moving to opposite poles, a dynamic event that helps students learn about distribution. Finally, Telophase reverses these steps, forming two new nuclei, allowing the cells to learn and divide successfully.",
      "source": "target",
