    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Prophase initiates the process where the loosely packed chromatin coils and condenses into distinct, visible chromosomes, allowing students to learn the structural changes necessary for accurate division. In Metaphase, the spindle fibers attach to the centromeres, aligning every chromosome precisely along the cell's metaphase plate, which helps students learn the critical organization required for successful replication. Anaphase is characterized by the separation of sister chromatids, which are pulled by the spindle apparatus toward opposite poles, enabling students to learn how genetic material is equally distributed. Lastly, Telophase involves the reformation of nuclear envelopes around the two separate sets of chromosomes, completing the process so that the resulting daughter cells can learn and function independently.",
      "source": "target",
